  {:name "Tendon"
   :tissue-type :tendon
   :model {:type :linear-elastic
           ;; 1.2 GPa IS THE SOURCE'S OWN NUMBER, not a value that happens to
           ;; sit inside a band. Maganaris & Paul 1999 report the Young's
           ;; modulus of human tibialis anterior tendon in vivo as 1.2 GPa at
           ;; maximum isometric load. The value carried here predates the
           ;; citation and is unchanged.
           :youngs-modulus 1.2e9
           ;; NOT SOURCED. No source read here measured a Poisson's ratio for
           ;; tendon; 0.40 is inherited from the uncited era and is retained
           ;; only because removing it would hand the fea bridge its 0.3
           ;; default silently.
           :poissons-ratio 0.40
           ;; NOT SOURCED. Same status as the Poisson's ratio.
           :density 1100
           :directional
           {:basis "Maganaris & Paul 1999, human tibialis anterior tendon in vivo, five males, neutral ankle. The tendon was loaded along its long axis by percutaneous stimulation of the muscle; there is one direction in this experiment."
            :not-measured "Transverse and shear moduli. A tendon loaded across its fibres is a different and far lower number, and no source read here measured it."}}
   :source "Maganaris CN & Paul JP 1999 J Physiol 521(Pt 1):307-313 (PMID 10562354): Young's modulus 1.2 GPa. SPECIMEN: human tibialis anterior tendon, IN VIVO, five males, neutral ankle position; tendon force from joint moment / MRI moment arm, elongation from real-time ultrasonography. DIRECTION: along the tendon long axis, in tension. RATE: quasi-static ramped isometric contraction driven by increasing percutaneous stimulation voltage, not impact. REGIME: this is the modulus AT MAXIMUM ISOMETRIC LOAD (stress 25 MPa, strain 2.5%), and the paper's own conclusion is that even there the tendon is still inside the nonlinear 'toe' region -- so 1.2 GPa is a secant modulus at one physiological load, not the linear-region modulus of a tendon stretched to failure."
   :provenance
   {:scalar :sourced
    :sources
    [{:citation "Maganaris CN, Paul JP. In vivo human tendon mechanical properties. J Physiol. 1999;521(Pt 1):307-313."
      :doi "10.1111/j.1469-7793.1999.00307.x"
      :pmid "10562354"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :read "Verbatim: \"The tendon force and displacement at maximum isometric load were 530 N and 4.1 mm, and the corresponding stress and strain values were 25 MPa and 2.5 %, respectively. The tendon stiffness and Young's modulus at maximum isometric load were 161 N mm-1 and 1.2 GPa, respectively.\" And the regime sentence, verbatim: \"These results are in agreement with previous reports on in vitro testing of isolated tendons and suggest that under physiological loading the TA tendon operates within the elastic 'toe' region.\" Method, verbatim: \"Measurements were taken in five males at the neutral ankle position\" and involved isometric dynamometry with percutaneous electrical stimulation, real-time ultrasonography for tendon origin displacement and cross-sectional area, and MRI for tendon length and moment arm."
      :caveat "A tendon still inside its toe region has a modulus that RISES with load, so 1.2 GPa is a point on a rising curve and not a ceiling. n = 5, one tendon, one joint angle, one species, and the modulus is a secant value at the single load the stimulator could reach -- it is not the linear-region modulus of a tendon pulled to failure, and it must not be read as one."}]}}

  {:name "Cartilage"
   :tissue-type :cartilage
   ;; :biphasic, NOT :hyperelastic. The previous tag asserted that this tissue
   ;; is nonlinearly stiffening, and no source said so. Keenan 2009 fits a
   ;; LINEAR biphasic model -- solid matrix plus interstitial fluid, three
   ;; coefficients -- and those three coefficients are what this entry now
   ;; carries. Trading an unsourced constitutive claim for the one the source
   ;; actually used is the point.
   :model {:type :biphasic
           ;; THE OLD ENTRY WROTE AN AGGREGATE MODULUS INTO :youngs-modulus,
           ;; AND ITS OWN :source SAID SO: "articular cartilage compressive
           ;; aggregate modulus approx 0.5-1.5 MPa". That is the category error
           ;; kotoba.biomech.tissue/aggregate-modulus exists to name -- H_A is
           ;; the equilibrium stiffness of a laterally CONFINED specimen and is
           ;; a different experiment from a Young's modulus.
           ;;
           ;; THE CONFLATION SURVIVED BECAUSE OF A COINCIDENCE, AND THE
           ;; COINCIDENCE IS ITSELF SOURCED. For an isotropic linear elastic
           ;; solid, H_A = E(1-nu) / ((1+nu)(1-2nu)). At Keenan's measured
           ;; nu = 0.05 the factor is 0.95 / (1.05 * 0.90) = 1.0053, so H_A and
           ;; E differ by 0.53% -- roughly 100x smaller than the 0.48-1.58 MPa
           ;; spread Keenan measured across five sites of ONE tibial plateau.
           ;; Writing 8.0e5 in both fields is therefore defensible to the
           ;; precision the source supports. They remain different quantities,
           ;; and they now have different field names.
           :youngs-modulus 8.0e5
           :aggregate-modulus 8.0e5
           :aggregate-modulus-range [4.8e5 1.58e6]
           ;; 0.40 -> 0.05. THE LARGEST CORRECTION IN THIS FILE, AND THE OLD
           ;; VALUE CITED NOBODY. Keenan 2009 measured Poisson's ratio
           ;; 0.00-0.05 in human cartilage; 0.05 is the HIGH end of that band
           ;; and is taken here for the same reason the nucleus takes the low
           ;; end of its |G*| band -- a named endpoint of a measured range is
           ;; readable, a midpoint of it is invented.
           ;;
           ;; DIRECTION OF THE ERROR, STATED. Isotropic bulk modulus is
           ;; K = E / (3(1-2nu)). At nu = 0.40 that is 1.667 E; at nu = 0.05 it
           ;; is 0.370 E. The old value made cartilage 4.5x more resistant to
           ;; volume change than the only measurement read here says it is. A
           ;; near-zero Poisson's ratio is not an oddity of this dataset: it is
           ;; what a biphasic fit reports, because at equilibrium the fluid has
           ;; left and the drained solid matrix barely resists volume change.
           :poissons-ratio 0.05
           :poissons-ratio-range [0.0 0.05]
           ;; Keenan's third coefficient. The LOW end of 1.7-5.4 x 10^-15
           ;; m^4/(N*s), named rather than averaged, on the same convention as
           ;; the modulus above.
           :permeability 1.7e-15
           :permeability-range [1.7e-15 5.4e-15]
           ;; NOT SOURCED. No source read here weighed cartilage.
           :density 1100
           :sites
           {:basis "Keenan 2009: creep indentation at five sites across ONE human tibial plateau. The 0.48-1.58 MPa spread is BETWEEN THOSE FIVE SITES, not between donors -- a threefold range inside a single joint surface."
            :per-site-values :not-in-abstract
            :independent-agreement "Athanasiou 1991 measured the same three coefficients by the same method across five species and found site to matter in a stated direction: the anterior patellar groove has the LOWEST aggregate modulus, the HIGHEST permeability and the LOWEST Poisson's ratio of all sites tested. Its abstract publishes no number, so no value here comes from it -- but it is an independent statement that one scalar cannot describe this tissue across a joint."}}
   :source "Keenan KE et al. 2009 Comput Methods Biomech Biomed Engin 12(4):415-422 (PMID 19675978): human cartilage aggregate modulus 0.48-1.58 MPa, Poisson's ratio 0.00-0.05, permeability 1.7-5.4 x 10^-15 m^4/(N*s). SPECIMEN: five sites across a human tibial plateau, in vitro (species and joint confirmed from the PubMed MeSH record -- Humans, Knee Joint, Tibia, In Vitro Techniques -- not from the abstract text). LOADING: creep indentation, fitted with a linear biphasic finite-element interpolant; the modulus is therefore an EQUILIBRIUM confined stiffness, not a transient or impact response. RATE: quasi-static creep to equilibrium. CHANGED HERE: :poissons-ratio 0.40 -> 0.05, because 0.40 cited nobody and 0.00-0.05 is what was measured; and the aggregate modulus now has its own field instead of masquerading as a Young's modulus."
   :provenance
   {:scalar :unsourced-but-bounded
    :sources
    [{:citation "Keenan KE, Kourtis LC, Besier TF, Lindsey DP, Gold GE, Delp SL, Beaupre GS. New resource for the computation of cartilage biphasic material properties with the interpolant response surface method. Comput Methods Biomech Biomed Engin. 2009;12(4):415-422."
      :doi "10.1080/10255840802654319"
      :pmid "19675978"
      :pmcid "PMC2858459"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :read "Verbatim: \"Creep indentation tests were performed on five sites across a tibial plateau.\" And: \"These sites provided a representative range of aggregate moduli (0.48-1.58 MPa), Poisson's ratio (0.00-0.05) and permeability (1.7 x 10(- 15)-5.4 x 10(- 15) m(4)/N s) values found in human cartilage.\" Method, verbatim: \"Three biphasic material properties (aggregate modulus, Poisson's ratio and permeability) can be determined using an analytical or finite element model combined with optimisation to find the material properties values that best reproduce an experimental creep curve.\""
      :caveat "THE 8.0e5 CARRIED HERE IS NOBODY'S MEASUREMENT. It sits inside 0.48-1.58 MPa and is unchanged from the uncited era, exactly as the ligament's 5.0e8 sits inside Neumann's 423-1095 MPa. The abstract gives a RANGE ACROSS FIVE SITES OF ONE PLATEAU and no mean, so there is no single number to adopt; picking a midpoint would invent one. A PMC identifier exists but the Europe PMC fullTextXML endpoint returns 404 for it, so the per-site table was not read."}]
    :unobtained
    [{:citation "Athanasiou KA, Rosenwasser MP, Buckwalter JA, Malinin TI, Mow VC. Interspecies comparisons of in situ intrinsic mechanical properties of distal femoral cartilage. J Orthop Res. 1991;9(3):330-340."
      :doi "10.1002/jor.1100090304"
      :pmid "2010837"
      :could-not-obtain :numbers-not-in-abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :note "The abstract was read in full and is entirely qualitative on the quantities this entry carries. It is corroborating and directional: \"The aggregate modulus of the anterior patellar groove within each species is the lowest among all sites tested, and the permeability of the patellar groove cartilage is the highest and does not vary among species. Similarly, the Poison's ratio in the patellar groove is the lowest in all species\", and \"For any given location, no differences were found in the aggregate modulus among all the species\". That last clause is the one worth keeping: it says a bovine or canine number is not automatically wrong for human cartilage AT THE SAME SITE -- and that the site is what matters. NO NUMBER IN THIS ENTRY COMES FROM ATHANASIOU."}]}}

  {:name "Ligament"
   :tissue-type :ligament
   :model {:type :linear-elastic
           :youngs-modulus 5.0e8
           :poissons-ratio 0.40
           :density 1100}
   :source "Neumann 1992 J Biomech 25(10):1185-1194 (PMID 1400518): ALL \"overall\" tensile modulus 759 MPa (S.D. 336), i.e. 423-1095 MPa. The 5.0e8 Pa carried here sits inside that band and is unchanged. DIRECTION: along the fibre axis, in tension; a ligament loaded across its fibres is a different and much lower number this entry does not carry. RATE: ~1.0% strain per second (2.5 mm/s crosshead); ligament is rate-dependent and this does not describe an impact."
   :provenance
   {:sources
    [{:citation "Neumann P, Keller TS, Ekstrom L, Perry L, Hansson TH, Spengler DM. Mechanical properties of the human lumbar anterior longitudinal ligament. J Biomech. 1992;25(10):1185-1194."
      :doi "10.1016/0021-9290(92)90074-b"
      :pmid "1400518"
      :obtained :abstract
      :read "Verbatim: \"The average tensile strength, the 'overall' tensile modulus and the 'overall' strain of the ALL at failure were 27.4 MPa (S.D. 5.9), 759 MPa (S.D. 336) and 4.95% (S.D. 1.51), respectively.\" Bone-ALL-bone specimens from young human cadaveric motion segments, distracted to failure at 2.5 mm/s (\"approximately 1.0% strain per second\")."
      :caveat "759 MPa is an \"overall\" modulus of a bone-ligament-bone complex, not a mid-substance material modulus, and the paper's own result is that the strain field is not uniform: \"Peak substance strains were over twofold greater than peak strains at the ligament insertion sites\" and across the width \"peak strains in the outer portion of the ligament were over 40% greater than in the central region\". A single modulus averages that away. This is the ANTERIOR LONGITUDINAL ligament specifically; it is used here as the representative spinal ligament because it is the one whose modulus could actually be read."}]
    :unobtained
    [{:citation "Pintar FA, Yoganandan N, Myers T, Elhagediab A, Sances A. Biomechanical properties of human lumbar spine ligaments. J Biomech. 1992;25(11):1351-1356."
      :doi "10.1016/0021-9290(92)90290-h"
      :pmid "1400536"
      :could-not-obtain :numbers-not-in-abstract
      :note "This is the source that would settle the per-ligament moduli. The abstract confirms six lumbar ligaments (ALL, PLL, joint capsules, ligamentum flavum, interspinous, supraspinous) from 38 cadavers and 132 samples, grouped into T12-L2 / L2-L4 / L4-S1, and states \"A total of 18 data curves are presented\". It publishes NO numeric modulus in the abstract; the curves and the derived failure stress/strain are in the full text, which was not read. So the ligamentum flavum, interspinous and supraspinous moduli are NOT carried here. That matters to a specific consumer: cloud-itonami/suji bundles exactly those three (supraspinous + interspinous + thoracolumbar fascia) as its posterior_lumbar_ligaments, and this entry cannot supply its modulus."}]}}

